---
/**
 * Home — `optfall.com`.
 *
 * `docs/DESIGN.md`, Screen 1: "One search field, one hint line, one daily
 * object", and the principle it inherits — "No marketing hero, no illustration
 * above the fold. The first thing on the page is the thing you came to do."
 *
 * So the fold holds the mark, the field and one hint, and nothing else. The
 * explanation of what Optfall is lives BELOW it deliberately: a reference tool
 * that explains itself before letting you search has misunderstood what it is.
 * The daily chain link — the third object Screen 1 names — is absent rather
 * than faked; it is keyed on the interaction corpus, which does not exist yet,
 * and a placeholder for it would be the marketing hero this page exists to
 * refuse.
 *
 * ONE HINT, AND IT IS NOT THIS FILE'S. The hint teaches by example rather than
 * by documentation, because people arrive already fluent — `docs/DESIGN.md`
 * puts one inherited Card Vault operator beside ours, so the extension is
 * discovered where the fluency already is. All of that wording now lives in
 * `RulesSearch`, on the line that is wired to the field by `aria-describedby`.
 * This page used to print a second hint of its own *after* the island, which
 * put two hint lines on one screen and left the one teaching the extension ten
 * rows below the field it described. One field, one hint; if the hint needs
 * changing, change it where the field is.
 *
 * ORNAMENT MARKS THE END OF THE FOLD, SO THE ISLAND PLACES IT. The chapter
 * browse renders inside `RulesSearch`, which means an ornamented rule written
 * here can only land after it — below the fold it was supposed to terminate.
 * `ornament` on the island puts it between the hint and the browse instead,
 * where the fold actually ends. The plain rule further down is the ordinary
 * hairline that introduces the prose, and carries no filigree: scrollwork is
 * rationed to once a screen.
 *
 * COMPOSE, NEVER RESTYLE. The mark and the rule are primitives from
 * `optfall-components`; the search island is its own component; the layout owns
 * typography, the measure and the focus ring. What is left here is the stacking
 * of those pieces, and every value in it names a `--of-*` token — the `tokens`
 * CI job fails the build otherwise, including on a `var()` naming a token that
 * does not exist.
 */
import { Mark, OrnamentalRule } from "optfall-components/svelte";

import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import NoScriptNotice from "../components/NoScriptNotice.astro";
import RulesSearch from "../components/RulesSearch.svelte";
import { buildIndex, type RulesCorpus } from "../lib/search";
import corpusJson from "../../../../data/rules/cr-2.14.0.json?raw";
// Build-time only — this is frontmatter, not an island, so the 16 MB card
// corpus is read on the build machine and none of it reaches a browser.
import { CORPUS as CARDS } from "../lib/cards";

/**
 * The corpus is read here, at build time, and the index is built from it — the
 * same shape `/search` uses, deliberately: one way to load the corpus, so the
 * two surfaces cannot disagree about what is in it. This frontmatter runs
 * during `astro build` and never again; there is no server to run it on.
 *
 * `?raw` rather than `readFileSync`, for the reason `search.astro` records: a
 * relative path handed to `readFileSync` resolves against the *bundled* module
 * during `astro build`, not against this source file, so it points at a
 * directory that does not exist. That is not a theory — the first version of
 * this page did exactly that, and the only visible symptom was a fallback
 * string in `dist/index.html`.
 *
 * The full 651 KB of verbatim rules text never reaches a browser. `buildIndex`
 * reduces it to what ranking needs plus one line of context per section, and
 * the section text itself lives on the permalink page, which is the thing the
 * field exists to send people to.
 */
const corpus = JSON.parse(corpusJson) as RulesCorpus;
const index = buildIndex(corpus);

/**
 * Stated rather than typed into the prose. "Degrade visibly" is a project-wide
 * rule — every surface shows when its data was last confirmed — and a version
 * and a count copied by hand are two more things to rot at the next release.
 */
const sections = corpus.sections.length.toLocaleString("en-GB");
const cards = CARDS.counts.cards.toLocaleString("en-GB");
const published = new Date(`${corpus.publishedDateIso}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString(
  "en-GB",
  { day: "numeric", month: "long", year: "numeric", timeZone: "UTC" },
);
const bulkExport =
  "https://github.com/alxjrvs/optfall/blob/main/data/rules/cr-2.14.0.json";
---

<BaseLayout title="Optfall">
  <!-- The mark is decorative here: the wordmark beside it already carries the
       name, and announcing it twice is noise rather than information. -->
  <div class="wordmark">
    <Mark size="lg" decorative />
    <h1>Optfall</h1>
  </div>

  <!-- Above the field, because it explains the field. Renders for nobody with
       scripting on, and for everybody without it. -->
  <NoScriptNotice example="/search?q=dominate">Every section is addressable without it: <code>/cr/8.3.4b</code> is the section cited as <code>cr:8.3.4b</code>, and the chapter list under the field is nine links into the same corpus.</NoScriptNotice>

  <!--
    `client:load`, not `client:visible` or `client:idle`. On this page the field
    is the only thing that matters, and a hero that is inert for the first
    keystrokes is a broken hero.

    `ornament` spends the screen's one filigree on the island's own section
    rule, which is the only position where it sits between the hint and the
    chapter browse — the point where the fold ends.
  -->
  <RulesSearch index={index} ornament client:load />

  <!-- The ordinary hairline that introduces the prose, and deliberately not a
       second ornament: filigree is rationed to three roles and never twice on a
       page. No `label`: the break is already announced by its role and named by
       the heading beneath it, and a name here would be invented text read aloud
       for nothing. -->
  <OrnamentalRule />

  <h2>What this is</h2>

  <p>
    A reference tool for Flesh and Blood that optimises for the one thing no incumbent optimises for: being <strong>right</strong>. Correct rules text, correct legality, correct rulings — each citable, each version-stamped, each with a permanent URL you can paste into an argument instead of describing which paragraph you meant.
  </p>

  <p>
    No language model is involved in anything Optfall serves. Search is lexical and deterministic, and every string traces to a parsed official document or a named human author — so a confidently wrong ruling is not a risk to manage but a thing the tool cannot do.
  </p>

  <h2>What is built</h2>

  <p>
    The Comprehensive Rules, <strong>{corpus.version}</strong>, published {published} by Legend Story Studios and parsed to {sections} addressable sections — searchable above, and each one on its own permanent URL. The identifier is LSS's own numbering rather than a slug of ours, so <a href="/cr/1.0.1a"><code>cr:1.0.1a</code></a> means here exactly what it means in the PDF.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>{cards}</strong> cards, pinned to an upstream commit and synced rather than curated, each on its own permanent URL with its printed text, its printings and its per-format legality — and every legality verdict prints the upstream flags it was derived from, because <a href="/card/winters-wail">a card can be two things at once</a> and a tool that collapses that is a tool that is wrong. <a href="/cards">Search the cards</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not yet: legality as of a date, the deck checker, version diffs, and the judge-attributed interaction record. Nothing above hides behind a placeholder — if a surface is not here, it is not built.
  </p>

  <p>
    The corpus is not only a website. It ships as an openly licensed <a href={bulkExport}>bulk export</a>, read from <a href={corpus.sourceUrl}>the published document</a>, so if this site disappears the corpus does not. The plan and the design language are public too — <a href="https://github.com/alxjrvs/optfall/blob/main/docs/PLAN.md">the phased build plan</a> and <a href="https://github.com/alxjrvs/optfall/blob/main/docs/DESIGN.md">the design pass</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /*
    Stacking only. Nothing here draws anything: no colour of its own beyond the
    muted ink the hint already owes the token layer, no box, no shadow, no
    border — the system holds a page together with typography and hairlines
    rather than with cards and padding.
  */
  .wordmark {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--of-space-base);
    /* The fold starts at the top of the page. The layout's own heading rhythm
       would push the field down behind a title, which is the marketing hero
       this screen refuses. */
    margin-block-end: var(--of-space-looser);
  }

  .wordmark h1 {
    margin-block: 0;
  }
</style>
